WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100 position:63%
- At the Lumière Place
Casino in St. Louis,

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.590 position:63%
a man holds his phone
up to a slot machine,

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.590 position:63%
and he records the spins on the screen.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.940 position:63%
He played a few rounds.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.830 position:63%
He won some, lost some,

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.110 position:63%
and then he got up and walked away.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.100 position:63%
Five minutes later,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.620 position:63%
he was back playing the same machine,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.838 position:63%
but now he was winning on spin after spin.

00:00:19.838 --> 00:00:21.960 position:63%
(machine chiming)
(coins clattering)

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.730 position:63%
After less than an hour of gambling,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.232 position:63%
he walked out of the casino
roughly $10,000 richer.

00:00:27.232 --> 00:00:28.140 position:63%
(dramatic music)

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.120 position:63%
At least 10 casinos across

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.980 position:63%
Missouri, Illinois, and California

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.480 position:63%
saw the same thing.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.700 position:63%
Gamblers would play on a
slot machine for a while

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.680 position:63%
and then leave, and minutes later,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.873 position:63%
they would return and start winning.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.240 position:63%
Investigators were brought in,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.280 position:63%
but when they examined the slot machines,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.800 position:63%
they all seemed to be working fine.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.750 position:63%
So they turned their attention

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:49.950 position:63%
to the security footage,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.410 position:63%
and they noticed something strange.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.760 position:63%
When these players came back,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.980 position:63%
they didn't play like normal gamblers.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.000 position:63%
They'd hover their hand
over the spin button, wait,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.050 position:63%
and then press at oddly specific moments

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.900 position:63%
as if they knew when to press to win.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.400 position:63%
And maybe that makes sense.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.980 position:63%
Perhaps there's some way to
predict what was coming next

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.800 position:63%
because the outcomes of
those spinning wheels

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.040 position:63%
are actually decided by a
computer inside the machine.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.170 position:63%
Now gambling is a terrible idea,

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.330 position:63%
but the casino provides an ideal venue

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.250 position:63%
for investigating randomness,

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.470 position:63%
because theoretically, that computer

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.900 position:63%
uses a random number for every spin.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.840 position:63%
So are those numbers actually random?

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.360 position:63%
Well, that seemingly simple question

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.180 position:63%
turns out to connect finding
diamonds in Minecraft,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.000 position:63%
to the development of the
first nuclear weapons,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.793 position:63%
to the security of the
entire digital world.

00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:45.300 position:63%
This is a video about
the near impossibility

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.123 position:63%
of creating true randomness.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.670 position:63%
Back in the 1940s,

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.530 position:63%
physicists discovered that chance

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.450 position:63%
plays a surprisingly important role

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.060 position:63%
in what happens inside a nuclear bomb.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.220 position:63%
It works something like this.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.770 position:63%
Take a core of uranium-235.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.130 position:63%
When a neutron hits a U-235 nucleus,

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.050 position:63%
it can cause the nucleus to split,

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.430 position:63%
which releases energy

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.830 position:63%
and around two or three more neutrons.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.740 position:63%
Now, if on average each split triggers

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.510 position:63%
more than one more split,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.118 position:63%
you get a runaway chain reaction.

00:02:18.118 --> 00:02:19.230 position:63%
(explosion roars)
(ground rumbling)

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.760 position:63%
But whether that happens

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.850 position:63%
depends on the path those neutrons take.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.380 position:63%
How far does each one travel

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.520 position:63%
before it hits a nucleus,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.950 position:63%
or does it bounce off, or get absorbed,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.693 position:63%
or does it cause another to split?

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.530 position:63%
By 1945, scientists working at Los Alamos

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.980 position:63%
had measured the odds of each
of these events happening,

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.070 position:63%
but those odds changed
depending on things like

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.620 position:63%
the neutron's speed and
what it was passing through.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.040 position:63%
And across trillions and
trillions of neutrons,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.720 position:63%
there were far too many possibilities

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.396 position:63%
to calculate one by one.

00:02:50.396 --> 00:02:51.450 position:63%
(dramatic music)

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.640 position:63%
One of the mathematicians
working on this problem

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.560 position:63%
was Stanislaw Ulam.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.590 position:63%
But then one morning in January 1946,

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.710 position:63%
Ulam woke up and couldn't speak.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.060 position:63%
It was encephalitis,

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.060 position:63%
an inflammation of the brain
that nearly killed him.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.380 position:63%
Luckily, he survived,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.210 position:63%
but his recovery was slow.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.550 position:63%
So he spent weeks in a hospital playing

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.320 position:63%
a lot of solitaire,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.767 position:63%
and he started to wonder,

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:17.759 position:63%
"What are the chances of winning a game?"

00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:18.750 position:63%
(light curious music)

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.150 position:63%
Now, at first, that might
sound like a simple question,

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:25.680 position:63%
but a deck of 52 cards can be
shuffled in 52 factorial ways,

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.980 position:63%
and that's about 8 times
10 to the power of 67

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.240 position:63%
different arrangements.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.530 position:63%
And every one of those shuffles

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.000 position:63%
is going to play out differently.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.850 position:63%
Now, Ulam spent a lot of
time trying to work out

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.890 position:63%
the answer to his question analytically,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.510 position:63%
and he got nowhere.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.187 position:63%
But then he had a much simpler idea.

00:03:42.187 --> 00:03:44.917 position:63%
"I wondered whether a
more practical method

00:03:44.917 --> 00:03:47.887 position:63%
"might not be to lay
it out, say, 100 times

00:03:47.887 --> 00:03:50.197 position:63%
"and simply observe and count the number

00:03:50.197 --> 00:03:52.380 position:63%
"of successful plays."

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:53.730 position:63%
That would give him some sort of

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.250 position:63%
statistical approximation of the answer.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.800 position:63%
So say you win 8 games out of a 100.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.020 position:63%
Well, then your overall chances of winning

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.690 position:63%
are probably around 8%.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.190 position:63%
And that's when Ulam realized

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.230 position:63%
he could use exactly the same idea,

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.250 position:63%
but for neutrons.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.810 position:63%
So instead of trying to calculate

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.190 position:63%
every possible path,

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.410 position:63%
he could just start
with a hundred neutrons

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.330 position:63%
and use random numbers to simulate

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.010 position:63%
what would happen to each of them.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.600 position:63%
So say a neutron hits an atom.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.540 position:63%
If I roll a one, a two, or a three,

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.010 position:63%
it bounces off the nucleus.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.720 position:63%
If I roll a four or a five,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.520 position:63%
it causes the nucleus to split.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.400 position:63%
And if I roll a six, it gets absorbed.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.920 position:63%
And then I just follow what
happens to that neutron

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.140 position:63%
and any other neutrons that it produces

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.370 position:63%
using more random numbers,

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.990 position:63%
and that will decide their fate.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.730 position:63%
Now those are just example odds.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:39.840 position:63%
In the real simulations,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.400 position:63%
the scientists used probabilities

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.350 position:63%
they had already measured to decide

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.910 position:63%
which random numbers corresponded

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.320 position:63%
to which outcomes.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.700 position:63%
And after each interaction,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:48.840 position:63%
the neutron's speed

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.520 position:63%
or direction could also change.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:51.660 position:63%
So for the next step,

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.160 position:63%
they'd used the probabilities

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.420 position:63%
for its new situation.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.430 position:63%
So use another random number,

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.710 position:63%
and then they kept
following what happened.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.240 position:63%
And by repeating this process,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.750 position:63%
they could see how all
those interactions added up.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.820 position:63%
If the number of neutrons keeps growing,

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.353 position:63%
the chain reaction takes off.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.160 position:63%
But if more are lost than are produced,

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.300 position:63%
it fizzles out.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.320 position:63%
So by simulating just a tiny
fraction of the possible paths,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.050 position:63%
Ulam could estimate how an
entire nuclear chain reaction

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:19.883 position:63%
would behave.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.670 position:63%
But doing all of these
calculations by hand

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:24.990 position:63%
seemed impossible.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.580 position:63%
Luckily, fellow mathematician

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.980 position:63%
and Los Alamos advisor John von Neumann

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.660 position:63%
had been working with one of the world's

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.870 position:63%
first electronic computers, the ENIAC.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.430 position:63%
In March 1947,

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.130 position:63%
he worked out how it
could follow 100 neutrons

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.140 position:63%
and their descendants through thousands

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.140 position:63%
of collisions in just five hours.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.300 position:63%
But the ENIAC couldn't roll a die

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.280 position:63%
to decide what would happen to a neutron.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.120 position:63%
At the time,

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.490 position:63%
when researchers needed random numbers,

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.850 position:63%
they used books of random
digits like this one.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:57.060 position:63%
And the ENIAC didn't
have nearly enough memory

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:59.940 position:63%
to store all the random
numbers it would need either.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.820 position:63%
So von Neumann came up with
a surprisingly simple way

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.883 position:63%
for the ENIAC to generate
its own random numbers.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.250 position:63%
Let's say I want to generate

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.440 position:63%
a bunch of random four digit numbers.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.790 position:63%
So the way we're going to do this

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:13.620 position:63%
is I'm actually going to
start by choosing one,

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:14.640 position:63%
which is called the seed.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.290 position:63%
So let's try with 2,137.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.300 position:63%
So what I'm going to do next is square it,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.817 position:63%
and 2,137 squared is 4,566,769.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.020 position:63%
Now I'm going to take the four

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:32.430 position:63%
middle digits of this number,

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:33.570 position:63%
but to do that first,

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.040 position:63%
I have to add a zero to the front

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.070 position:63%
because we initially had
a seven digit number.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.690 position:63%
I won't use the digits at the edges

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.250 position:63%
because they're actually more predictable.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.020 position:63%
So the last two digits

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:45.090 position:63%
only depend on the end of the seed

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.960 position:63%
because 37 squared ends in 1,369.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.420 position:63%
So anything ending in 37,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.060 position:63%
squares to something ending in 69.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.610 position:63%
So that's why we get
rid of these guys here.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.000 position:63%
Also, every seed in the
2,100s is going to square

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.060 position:63%
to somewhere between 4.4 and 4.9 million.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.280 position:63%
So they all start with a zero and a four,

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.840 position:63%
so we can get rid of that too.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.700 position:63%
But the middle digits get a contribution

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.410 position:63%
from every digit in the seed.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:15.240 position:63%
So 5,667 is going to be the
next number in the sequence.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.240 position:63%
So if we square that, we get 32,114,889.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.090 position:63%
Now I disregard the two
digits at either end,

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.477 position:63%
and you can just keep going.

00:07:28.477 --> 00:07:31.060 position:63%
(upbeat music)

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:33.990 position:63%
Looking at these numbers,

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:35.670 position:63%
there's no obvious pattern,

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.740 position:63%
but watch what happens if I keep going.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.560 position:63%
Starting from 2,137,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.280 position:63%
after 43 steps, I get 4,100,

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.197 position:63%
then 8,100,

00:07:46.197 --> 00:07:48.989 position:63%
and then 6,100,

00:07:48.989 --> 00:07:50.490 position:63%
and then 2,100,

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.590 position:63%
and then 4,100 again.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.530 position:63%
The sequence gets stuck in this loop.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.210 position:63%
From here on, the generator will produce

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.630 position:63%
these same four numbers
over and over forever,

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:04.110 position:63%
and nearly two-thirds
of all four digit seeds

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.486 position:63%
end up in this exact loop.

00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:08.400 position:63%
(dramatic music)

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:09.630 position:63%
Others end up at zero,

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:13.920 position:63%
and once they do every number
that follows is also zero.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:17.553 position:63%
Almost one in five starting
values end up here.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.480 position:63%
In fact, if you try every possible

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:22.950 position:63%
four digit starting value,

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.590 position:63%
every single sequence
eventually gets stuck

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.483 position:63%
in one of just eight traps.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:30.420 position:63%
Now, von Neumann knew that these traps

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:31.590 position:63%
were a serious problem.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.270 position:63%
So for the first ENIAC simulation,

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:36.300 position:63%
the team actually used eight
digit numbers, not four,

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.690 position:63%
so they produced much longer
sequences before they looped.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:42.390 position:63%
They also statistically
tested the first 2,000 numbers

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.010 position:63%
for randomness by hand.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.100 position:63%
Then rather than risk running
into an unexpected loop,

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.310 position:63%
they programmed the ENIAC to
restart from the original seed

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.400 position:63%
after it had got through
these 2,000 numbers.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.560 position:63%
And in the spring of 1948,

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.960 position:63%
the ENIAC finally ran the first

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.930 position:63%
computerized simulations
of neutron behavior.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.437 position:63%
Von Neumann wrote to Ulam,

00:09:02.437 --> 00:09:04.147 position:63%
"The results are very promising,

00:09:04.147 --> 00:09:07.617 position:63%
"and the method is
clearly a 100% success."

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.460 position:63%
The approach became known
as the Monte Carlo method,

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:13.260 position:63%
named after Monte Carlo, Monaco,

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.480 position:63%
where Ulam's uncle liked to gamble.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.850 position:63%
Today, it's one of the
most important tools

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.983 position:63%
in statistics and scientific computing.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.780 position:63%
Now, this sequence of
numbers isn't truly random.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:26.610 position:63%
If you start with the same seed,

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.580 position:63%
you get exactly the same
sequence every time.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.040 position:63%
So they're called pseudorandom numbers,

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.800 position:63%
but that didn't actually
stop them from being useful.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.530 position:63%
As Monte Carlo spread beyond Los Alamos,

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:40.320 position:63%
so did this demand for
pseudorandom numbers.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.037 position:63%
Still, as von Neumann joked,

00:09:42.037 --> 00:09:44.167 position:63%
"Anyone who considers arithmetical methods

00:09:44.167 --> 00:09:46.567 position:63%
"of producing random digits is, of course,

00:09:46.567 --> 00:09:48.720 position:63%
"in a state of sin."

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.040 position:63%
Because pseudorandom numbers

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.410 position:63%
came with three major challenges.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:56.100 position:63%
One, sequences could get
stuck in these short loops.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.000 position:63%
Two, even sequences that looked
random could hide patterns.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.070 position:63%
And three, if someone knew the seed,

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.440 position:63%
they could work out every
number that followed.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:05.540 position:63%
They were predictable.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:10.080 position:63%
- So, you got to show me how
we get these diamonds then.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:11.820 position:63%
- Yeah. All right.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.710 position:63%
- This is Matthew Bolan,

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.990 position:63%
Minecraft expert and
third-year PhD student

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:19.260 position:63%
in Mathematics at the
University of Toronto.

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.420 position:63%
Well, I shall follow you, sir.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.280 position:63%
If you haven't played Minecraft before,

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.200 position:63%
diamonds are a big deal.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.080 position:63%
I don't know if I've ever
made a full diamond set.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.060 position:63%
- We're going to change that today.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.240 position:63%
- Every Minecraft world
is made up of chunks.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.870 position:63%
They're segments 16 blocks
wide in the X direction

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.330 position:63%
and 16 blocks in the Z direction.

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:42.180 position:63%
And in our version, each chunk originates

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:45.060 position:63%
at most a single vein of diamond ore

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.349 position:63%
that is hidden somewhere
below the surface.

00:10:47.349 --> 00:10:48.580 position:63%
(bright music)

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.090 position:63%
Okay, so what are we looking for?

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.790 position:63%
- So we are looking for either

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:57.630 position:63%
a clay patch or a gravel patch.

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:00.120 position:63%
What you do is you pull up your F3 screen.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.120 position:63%
You locate which direction is positive Z.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.503 position:63%
So in this case, that's this way.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:12.090 position:63%
And what you do for clay, you add three.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:13.650 position:63%
So here, this was the center,

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:17.610 position:63%
and we go one, two,
three in the positive Z.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.743 position:63%
And now, if we dig down here,

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:24.990 position:63%
unless it got blocked
by bedrock or something,

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:26.700 position:63%
or there's like a cave down there

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.200 position:63%
where it couldn't spawn in,

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.600 position:63%
there will be diamond
ore under this block.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.070 position:63%
- Let's give it a shot.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:32.903 position:63%
- All right.

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:37.300 position:63%
- Oh, no way.

00:11:38.982 --> 00:11:40.502 position:63%
- Did you get anything?

00:11:40.502 --> 00:11:41.813 position:63%
- I did. I got eight.

00:11:41.813 --> 00:11:43.410 position:63%
- You got eight! Let's go!

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.699 position:63%
- I got eight. Oh, I'm a rich man.

00:11:45.699 --> 00:11:48.074 position:63%
Thank you, Matthew.
- Okay, okay.

00:11:48.074 --> 00:11:49.650 position:63%
- In this version of
Minecraft we're playing,

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.290 position:63%
the game uses an algorithm to generate

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:54.540 position:63%
the pseudorandom numbers that determine

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:58.140 position:63%
where resources like
clay and diamonds appear.

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:00.120 position:63%
It works something like this.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.850 position:63%
So I'm going to take a
wheel with seven positions.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:06.000 position:63%
It has numbers going
from zero through to six.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.280 position:63%
And what we're going to
do is we'll start at one

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.560 position:63%
and then multiply by some number.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:12.240 position:63%
In this case, say three.

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:13.980 position:63%
So one times three gives us three,

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.800 position:63%
so we move the arrow to three.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.340 position:63%
Now, we multiply three by
three again and we get nine,

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:23.070 position:63%
but there are only seven
positions on this wheel,

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:24.930 position:63%
so we're going to have to wrap around.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.540 position:63%
Essentially, what we're doing
is dividing nine by seven,

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.190 position:63%
and that gives us a remainder of two.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:32.640 position:63%
So we're going to wrap
around and go to two.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.220 position:63%
This kind of wrapping-around pattern

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:38.040 position:63%
is called modular arithmetic, or just mod.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:38.873 position:63%
And now we keep going.

00:12:38.873 --> 00:12:41.400 position:63%
So two times three is six.

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:43.260 position:63%
So we move to six.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:45.150 position:63%
And then 6 times 3 is 18.

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:46.890 position:63%
If you divide 18 by 7,

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:48.720 position:63%
you get two with the remainder of four.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.100 position:63%
So we get to four.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.540 position:63%
Four times 3 is 12.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:53.940 position:63%
The remainder after
dividing by seven is five.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:55.770 position:63%
Five times 3 is 15.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:58.470 position:63%
And the remainder after
dividing by seven is one.

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:00.600 position:63%
So what we've done is we've
gone through every number

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:03.480 position:63%
except for zero before
we started to repeat.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.780 position:63%
The formula for this kind of
algorithm looks like this.

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:08.640 position:63%
There's a simple linear relationship

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.580 position:63%
between the current number and the next,

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.280 position:63%
but the mod step keeps only the remainder

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:15.630 position:63%
after dividing by M.

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:18.450 position:63%
So instead of continuing
up one straight line,

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:20.040 position:63%
the values wrap around,

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.010 position:63%
falling along these parallel diagonals.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:26.550 position:63%
So it is a linear rule
using modular arithmetic,

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:28.320 position:63%
also called congruences.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.320 position:63%
So, these kinds of
pseudorandom number generators

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.470 position:63%
got the name linear
congruential generators,

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.183 position:63%
better known as LCGs.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:39.690 position:63%
With much larger numbers,

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.970 position:63%
LCGs can produce enormously long

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:43.950 position:63%
sequences of numbers before they repeat.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:45.300 position:63%
In the 1960s,

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.580 position:63%
IBM developed one called RANDU.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:50.190 position:63%
It was included in IBM's
scientific software

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:53.580 position:63%
and used in simulations of
everything from radioactive decay

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:57.180 position:63%
to instruments aboard
NASA's Skylab space station.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.710 position:63%
And RANDU could generate over

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:00.990 position:63%
half a billion numbers before repeating,

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.510 position:63%
so at least that was
one problem dealt with.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:06.990 position:63%
But a long sequence doesn't
necessarily mean a good one.

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:09.030 position:63%
Take a look at what happens if we take

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:10.440 position:63%
every three consecutive numbers

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.240 position:63%
and we plot them as coordinates

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.760 position:63%
in three-dimensional space, right?

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.590 position:63%
If the numbers behaved independently,

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:18.570 position:63%
we'd expect the points to fill the cube

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.340 position:63%
kind of in a fuzzy cloud.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:21.810 position:63%
And from this angle,

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:22.860 position:63%
they certainly seem to,

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:24.090 position:63%
and from most angles they do.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:26.160 position:63%
But if you rotate the cube

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:27.780 position:63%
in just the right way,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.300 position:63%
you can see that they actually line up

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.420 position:63%
in these 15 rows throughout the cube.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:34.710 position:63%
There are enormous gaps where

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:36.420 position:63%
RANDU will never place a point,

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:37.710 position:63%
and it turns out that that is just

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:39.960 position:63%
an unavoidable feature of all LCGs

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:42.750 position:63%
because of that linear relationship.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:44.970 position:63%
The second number is
calculated from the first

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:48.540 position:63%
and the third from the second
using exactly the same rule.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:52.290 position:63%
So all three numbers are
linked by a linear equation.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:53.640 position:63%
And in three dimensions,

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.040 position:63%
a linear equation describes a plane,

00:14:56.040 --> 00:15:00.510 position:63%
and the mod step creates a
series of parallel planes.

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:02.370 position:63%
RANDU's particular choice of numbers

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.590 position:63%
made that pattern especially obvious,

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:07.050 position:63%
but other LCGs are much better at this.

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:10.620 position:63%
In the 1990s, Java adopted
one that adds a constant

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:13.710 position:63%
using a better multiplier
and a much larger modulus.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:18.710 position:63%
Its sequence takes over 281
trillion steps before repeating.

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:21.360 position:63%
We can also plot its outputs the same way,

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:23.580 position:63%
and what we get is this.

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.170 position:63%
Now the planes are actually still there,

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.310 position:63%
but we can't see them

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:28.800 position:63%
because they're so closely packed together

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:32.040 position:63%
that the points more or less
look like a random cloud.

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:34.113 position:63%
So that is problem two dealt with.

00:15:36.473 --> 00:15:39.750 position:63%
(Gregor gasps)

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.520 position:63%
- Dude.
- Occupational hazard.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:43.650 position:63%
- This edition of
Minecraft is coded in Java.

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:45.000 position:63%
So for most of the game's history,

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.940 position:63%
it used Java's LCG to
determine the placement

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.440 position:63%
of resources in a given chunk.

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:53.190 position:63%
But when Minecraft generated
different resources,

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:55.980 position:63%
it didn't use completely
independent starting numbers.

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:58.680 position:63%
Instead, it took the same underlying seed

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:01.620 position:63%
and added a different fixed
number for each resource.

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.240 position:63%
So for diamonds and river clay,

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.300 position:63%
those starting numbers
differed by just three.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:08.850 position:63%
Java then modified those
seeds before using them,

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:11.370 position:63%
so the difference wasn't
always three anymore,

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:12.450 position:63%
but in most worlds,

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:15.720 position:63%
it fell into one of a
few predictable values.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:17.820 position:63%
And because they went
through the same LCG,

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:21.210 position:63%
the numbers used to choose
their position were related.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:22.380 position:63%
If you compare the results,

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:24.603 position:63%
the shared unknown part cancels out.

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.900 position:63%
So I didn't need to know the original seed

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:28.890 position:63%
of the Minecraft world.

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.090 position:63%
The location of the clay patch

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:32.340 position:63%
was enough to tell me where to look.

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.200 position:63%
Matthew has a great video on his channel

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.690 position:63%
that works through how
this works in more detail.

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:37.980 position:63%
We've linked it in the description

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:39.674 position:63%
if you want to go check it out.

00:16:39.674 --> 00:16:41.580 position:63%
(explosion rumbles)

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.770 position:63%
But there is a more fundamental problem.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:45.840 position:63%
The outputs themselves can reveal

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:49.260 position:63%
enough information to
predict what comes next.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.500 position:63%
See, Java's LCG keeps
track of an internal number

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:55.320 position:63%
that is made up of 48 binary digits,

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:57.690 position:63%
and this is what Java's LCG uses

00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:00.270 position:63%
to generate the next
number in the sequence.

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:03.000 position:63%
But when it generates an integer output,

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.490 position:63%
it actually reveals 32 of those digits,

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:07.830 position:63%
leaving just 16 hidden.

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:09.420 position:63%
And since it's binary,

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:11.820 position:63%
each can only be a zero or a one.

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:16.820 position:63%
So that's just 65,536 possible values.

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:18.600 position:63%
Now, maybe that sounds like a lot,

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:20.190 position:63%
but a computer can test them against

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:22.380 position:63%
the very next output of the generator,

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.270 position:63%
and only one matches.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.030 position:63%
And then you know the full
48-bit internal number,

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:28.140 position:63%
and once you know that,

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:31.320 position:63%
you can calculate every
number that follows.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:33.690 position:63%
And this is actually
the same for all LCGs.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.000 position:63%
They can produce incredibly long sequences

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.830 position:63%
with no obvious patterns,

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:40.170 position:63%
but their simple mathematical structure

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:41.883 position:63%
still makes them predictable.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:46.396 position:63%
- Like LCGs, they're not good.

00:17:46.396 --> 00:17:49.290 position:63%
They cause all sorts of problems.

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:50.250 position:63%
They're very exploitable.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.290 position:63%
They're for when you
have like a video game,

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:54.240 position:63%
and you just need
something quick and dirty,

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:56.310 position:63%
and you're not too worried
about people like me.

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:57.960 position:63%
- But I should point out
that this trick doesn't

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:01.440 position:63%
actually work on the most
recent versions of Minecraft.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:03.360 position:63%
- Once I published this trick,

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:06.360 position:63%
it got patched out of
the game very quickly.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.950 position:63%
- Oh, no way.

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:12.060 position:63%
- I think a bug report was
made in the version 1.18.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.460 position:63%
What they did is they actually switched

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:15.750 position:63%
the random number generator

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.660 position:63%
that Minecraft uses to generate ores

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:20.280 position:63%
to a different random number generator

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:22.110 position:63%
that would not have the particular flaw

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:23.730 position:63%
we're exploiting here.

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:26.310 position:63%
- They changed the whole number generator.

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:28.260 position:63%
- Yeah, exactly.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:29.770 position:63%
You can do the honors.

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.830 position:63%
- Okay, okay, okay.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:33.480 position:63%
Let's see if I can remember.

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:35.838 position:63%
Uh...
- (laughs) Oh, boy.

00:18:35.838 --> 00:18:36.846 position:63%
- Okay, check this out.

00:18:36.846 --> 00:18:38.250 position:63%
Here you go.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:40.260 position:63%
Pants, boots,

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.084 position:63%
chest piece, helmet.

00:18:42.084 --> 00:18:45.000 position:63%
(light upbeat music)

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:46.260 position:63%
The trick in Minecraft works

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:48.000 position:63%
because the developers chose to put

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.370 position:63%
this particular random number
generator into the game,

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.950 position:63%
but it's a good reminder of
how much the tools you use

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:54.990 position:63%
matter when you're building software.

