00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:06.900 position:63%
across the U.S.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:09.300 position:63%
- So when they picked these
guys up in California,

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.430 position:63%
they were able to get one of the phones,

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:13.620 position:63%
and they were able to see that the video

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:15.900 position:63%
was actually being transmitted

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:17.790 position:63%
back to a server in Russia.

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:20.340 position:63%
- More specifically, a
team in St. Petersburg,

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:23.940 position:63%
led by a hacker only known as Alex.

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:24.810 position:63%
Five years earlier,

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:27.060 position:63%
Russia actually banned
most of its gambling,

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:29.880 position:63%
and thousands of slot
machines were being sold off.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:32.610 position:63%
So Alex got hold of some of these machines

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.680 position:63%
and started taking them apart.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.480 position:63%
- There's a processing board,

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.370 position:63%
and then there's what
they refer to as a SMIB,

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:40.770 position:63%
which was the slot
machine interface board.

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:44.880 position:63%
Basically, the processing
board is locked into the game.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.443 position:63%
That comes with the machine.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.130 position:63%
Once you have access to the device,

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:51.600 position:63%
it's just a matter of time

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:53.820 position:63%
before it's reverse engineered.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:54.653 position:63%
- The thing is though,

00:20:54.653 --> 00:20:57.390 position:63%
these machines weren't
using an LCG like Java.

00:20:57.390 --> 00:20:59.220 position:63%
They used a far more complicated

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:00.570 position:63%
random number generator.

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:02.940 position:63%
- A lot of them were using the same

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:05.250 position:63%
Mersenne Twister algorithm.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:07.830 position:63%
- This algorithm still starts with a seed,

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:10.560 position:63%
but instead of using this
to generate one number,

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:14.790 position:63%
it fills a table of 624 numbers.

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:17.490 position:63%
It then performs a
calculation called a twist,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:19.470 position:63%
combining the binary digits from different

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:24.150 position:63%
parts of the table to
produce 624 new numbers.

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:26.400 position:63%
Then it takes the first
number in this new table,

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:28.860 position:63%
runs it through another
series of calculations,

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:31.923 position:63%
and it produces its first
pseudorandom output.

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.580 position:63%
It does the same with the
next number and the next

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:39.720 position:63%
until it has produced 624 outputs.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.700 position:63%
Then it twists the whole table again

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:43.050 position:63%
and the process continues,

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:44.760 position:63%
and its sequence is huge.

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:46.650 position:63%
The number of steps before it repeats

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:49.173 position:63%
has more than 6,000 digits.

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:53.610 position:63%
But despite all that,
every step still follow

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:55.560 position:63%
these fixed mathematical rules,

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.960 position:63%
and those were rules that Alex
was actually able to work out

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:00.930 position:63%
by studying the software
inside of his own machines.

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:03.390 position:63%
So when his team received videos

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:05.790 position:63%
of spins from casinos in the U.S.,

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.110 position:63%
they could work backwards,

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:09.540 position:63%
and they could compare them
with their own machines,

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:13.230 position:63%
and predict when a winning
spin was most likely.

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:14.850 position:63%
But inside the slot machines,

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:16.800 position:63%
the random number generator kept running

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:18.420 position:63%
even when nobody was playing,

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:20.580 position:63%
and the outcome depended
on the number selected

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:23.580 position:63%
at the instant the player pressed spin.

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:27.090 position:63%
So the gamblers had to press
at exactly the right time,

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:30.330 position:63%
but it takes a human roughly a
quarter of a second to react.

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:33.150 position:63%
So Alex built that delay into the system.

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.970 position:63%
He created an app that made
the gambler's phone vibrate

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:37.710 position:63%
a quarter of a second before

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:39.859 position:63%
they actually needed to press, and...

00:22:39.859 --> 00:22:43.320 position:63%
(machine chiming)
(coins clattering)

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:46.740 position:63%
Now, this trick didn't
guarantee a win on every spin,

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.900 position:63%
but a four person team could reportedly

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:52.440 position:63%
take more than $250,000 in a week,

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:54.720 position:63%
so it was pretty effective.

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:56.670 position:63%
In the end, the four men who were caught

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:58.200 position:63%
as part of the scheme were charged

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.300 position:63%
with conspiracy to commit fraud.

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:02.790 position:63%
Three of them were sentenced
to two years in prison,

00:23:02.790 --> 00:23:06.180 position:63%
while one chose to cooperate with the FBI.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.830 position:63%
Oh, but is it possible
that people are still

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:11.130 position:63%
running this exploit in casinos still?

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:15.300 position:63%
- Yeah, I would be completely
surprised if they weren't.

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:16.133 position:63%
- Oh, really?

00:23:16.133 --> 00:23:16.966 position:63%
- Yeah.

00:23:16.966 --> 00:23:19.500 position:63%
Yeah, there's a lot of those
boxes that are still out there.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:22.380 position:63%
- So ultimately, all of these
generators fail at being

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:25.140 position:63%
unpredictable if you know
enough about how they work

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:27.690 position:63%
and if you can study
enough of their outputs.

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:30.870 position:63%
But we need random numbers
for digital security.

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:32.220 position:63%
They're used to protect everything

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:34.140 position:63%
from our messages to our bank accounts,

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.060 position:63%
and if those numbers can be predicted,

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:38.313 position:63%
then that security can be compromised.

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:41.760 position:63%
So we went to the National Institute

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:43.140 position:63%
of Standards and Technology

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:44.490 position:63%
in Boulder, Colorado.

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:48.210 position:63%
NIST sets the standards for
secure random number generation,

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:52.140 position:63%
but one team there is trying
something even more ambitious.

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.390 position:63%
- So what we're trying to do is say,

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.670 position:63%
what's the best random number generator

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:59.370 position:63%
that nature allows you to build?

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:00.203 position:63%
- Yeah.

00:24:00.203 --> 00:24:02.160 position:63%
- And that's a lot of
what our work is here.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:03.960 position:63%
- [Gregor] And that's what this is about.

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.360 position:63%
- Yep, this is what all
of this experiment is

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:08.700 position:63%
to try to make the world's
best random number generator.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:12.510 position:63%
We really try to use physics and science

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:16.410 position:63%
to try to develop the best
measurements possible,

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:17.820 position:63%
and then we use those measurements

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:19.293 position:63%
to develop standards.

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:22.620 position:63%
- And that includes the standards

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:24.000 position:63%
for generating random numbers

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:25.170 position:63%
for digital security,

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:26.460 position:63%
because for encryption,

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:28.350 position:63%
you need generators that are much harder

00:24:28.350 --> 00:24:30.300 position:63%
to predict than the
ones we've seen so far.

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:32.220 position:63%
So cryptographers have developed

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:34.170 position:63%
a different kind of generator.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:36.420 position:63%
The idea behind them is pretty simple.

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:38.040 position:63%
Start with a string of numbers,

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:40.860 position:63%
say just one, two, three, four, and so on,

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:43.140 position:63%
and now you feed each of those numbers

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:44.580 position:63%
into an encryption algorithm.

00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:47.430 position:63%
The algorithm scrambles
them using a secret key,

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:50.370 position:63%
which is based on a large random number.

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:52.080 position:63%
Encryption transforms the inputs

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:54.690 position:63%
so thoroughly that without the key,

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:57.150 position:63%
the outputs look completely unrelated.

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:00.630 position:63%
In a way, that key plays
the role of a seed.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:02.790 position:63%
It's the one random
number you need to start,

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:05.910 position:63%
and it decides every
number that comes out.

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:08.400 position:63%
Where with Java, its outputs revealed

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:09.930 position:63%
most of its hidden state,

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:11.520 position:63%
and with the Mersenne Twister,

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:14.850 position:63%
even the extra steps hiding
its outputs could be reversed.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:18.240 position:63%
Here, even if you know exactly
how the encryption works,

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:20.610 position:63%
and you've seen all
its inputs and outputs,

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:23.673 position:63%
there's no practical way
to recover the secret key.

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:27.210 position:63%
And guessing it isn't realistic either.

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:30.360 position:63%
A common key length is 128 bits,

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:33.990 position:63%
so that's 128 ones and zeros and over

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:37.503 position:63%
300 trillion, trillion,
trillion possible keys.

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:39.683 position:63%
And without the key,

00:25:39.683 --> 00:25:41.190 position:63%
predicting the next output,

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:42.660 position:63%
it's practically impossible.

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:44.220 position:63%
This is one of the ways that you can build

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:46.980 position:63%
a cryptographically secure
pseudorandom number generator,

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:50.190 position:63%
and it solves our third
problem, predictability.

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:53.100 position:63%
But where did that secret
key actually come from?

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:56.220 position:63%
Because the algorithm can't
create unpredictability

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:57.570 position:63%
from nothing on its own.

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:59.940 position:63%
It actually needs something
random to start with.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.640 position:63%
And for that, computers
turn to the physical world.

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:05.550 position:63%
Many modern processors
have built-in hardware

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:08.940 position:63%
random number generators that
measure tiny fluctuations

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:12.243 position:63%
in electrical signals to provide
those unpredictable seeds.

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:15.300 position:63%
One company in California, Cloudflare,

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:17.910 position:63%
even uses a whole wall of lava lamps

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:20.110 position:63%
to generate random seeds
for its algorithms.

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:24.240 position:63%
So does that mean the problem is solved?

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:25.320 position:63%
Not exactly.

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:27.060 position:63%
If you take a coin toss,

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:28.410 position:63%
we normally think of this

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:31.860 position:63%
as a random event, heads or tails.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:34.530 position:63%
Only under carefully
controlled conditions,

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:35.940 position:63%
you can actually make the coin

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:37.860 position:63%
land heads every single time,

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.540 position:63%
and that's because a coin

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.880 position:63%
follows the laws of classical mechanics.

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:44.850 position:63%
So if you control exactly how it starts

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:46.470 position:63%
and how it's flipped,

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:49.830 position:63%
you can predict how it will
land every single time.

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:51.780 position:63%
The French mathematician
Pierre-Simon Laplace

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:53.970 position:63%
actually took this idea even further.

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:56.790 position:63%
He imagined an intelligence
that knew the position

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.790 position:63%
and motion of every
particle in the universe.

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:01.860 position:63%
So according to classical mechanics,

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:05.160 position:63%
it could calculate everything
that would ever happen.

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:07.110 position:63%
- You know, I looked long
and hard to try to figure out

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:09.420 position:63%
what is a good definition of randomness.

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:11.130 position:63%
And my definition of randomness

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:14.310 position:63%
is just something that nobody

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:16.380 position:63%
could have predicted in advance.

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:17.250 position:63%
And not just nobody,

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:18.180 position:63%
I don't mean just humans,

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:19.880 position:63%
but like anything in the universe.

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:22.500 position:63%
- [Gregor] So that's what Krister's team

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:23.760 position:63%
is trying to achieve,

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:26.040 position:63%
not just to generate
numbers that seem random,

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:29.640 position:63%
but to prove that they are
genuinely unpredictable.

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:31.260 position:63%
- We don't want to do seeds at all,

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:33.030 position:63%
and we don't want to do algorithms at all.

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:34.800 position:63%
- So they've turned to quantum mechanics

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:36.990 position:63%
because some quantum measurement outcomes

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:39.450 position:63%
are fundamentally unpredictable.

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:41.730 position:63%
- So on this setup right here,

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:46.170 position:63%
we start with a very bright
laser, a very intense laser.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:47.880 position:63%
You're generating about a hundred million

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:49.440 position:63%
of these pulses per second,

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.440 position:63%
and this pulse of light comes in

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:54.930 position:63%
and interacts with this crystal here.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:56.460 position:63%
What can happen is the interaction

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:59.430 position:63%
of this light with this crystal,

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:04.410 position:63%
sometimes one of our photons
will interact with it

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:08.066 position:63%
and fission into what we call
a pair of daughter photons.

00:28:08.066 --> 00:28:09.120 position:63%
(light dramatic music)

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:10.290 position:63%
- [Gregor] Now, each photon

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:12.480 position:63%
has a property called polarization,

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:14.040 position:63%
which corresponds to the direction

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:17.250 position:63%
in which light's electric
field oscillates.

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:19.500 position:63%
And the researchers have
arranged the experiment

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:22.440 position:63%
so that the pair can be
produced with both photons

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:24.390 position:63%
polarized either horizontally

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:25.950 position:63%
or both vertically,

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:28.320 position:63%
but there's no way to distinguish which.

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:31.200 position:63%
So the photons end up in
a quantum superposition

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:32.883 position:63%
of both possibilities.

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:36.210 position:63%
- This is very much like a
Schrödinger's cat-type state,

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.190 position:63%
but it extended to two particles,

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:40.020 position:63%
and now it's like they're kind of

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:42.330 position:63%
both of those at the same time.

00:28:42.330 --> 00:28:44.130 position:63%
- [Gregor] The photons
share a quantum state

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:46.140 position:63%
no matter where in the universe they are.

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:48.690 position:63%
In other words, they are entangled.

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:50.880 position:63%
And this means that when we measure both,

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:52.713 position:63%
the results are correlated.

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:55.650 position:63%
But first, they need to send the photons

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:58.140 position:63%
to two separate measurement stations,

00:28:58.140 --> 00:29:00.540 position:63%
and that means they
have to get each photon

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:01.953 position:63%
into an optical fiber.

00:29:03.030 --> 00:29:07.320 position:63%
- The really hard part is
these fiber cables right here.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.260 position:63%
The actual fiber optic cable
inside, you can't see it.

00:29:10.260 --> 00:29:13.350 position:63%
It's only about 10 microns in diameter.

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:15.750 position:63%
And to put that in context,

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:18.390 position:63%
that's about one-tenth the diameter

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:20.580 position:63%
or width of a human hair.

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:22.830 position:63%
So it's a very, very tiny target.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:24.990 position:63%
And what we have to do is

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.210 position:63%
what I like to call quantum archery.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:29.730 position:63%
So, you know, I think about Robin Hood

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:32.520 position:63%
and the fact that, you know,
he could kind of pull back

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:36.060 position:63%
and shoot an arrow and hit a
target dead in the bullseye.

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:37.320 position:63%
And then he can keep shooting arrows

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:40.860 position:63%
and keep splitting the
center of the arrow.

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:43.050 position:63%
We're doing a version of that

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.660 position:63%
where now Robin Hood has two targets,

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:46.860 position:63%
and he has a single bow.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:49.260 position:63%
And he has to shoot two arrows at a time,

00:29:49.260 --> 00:29:51.030 position:63%
and yet he's able to continually

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:53.220 position:63%
keep splitting these two targets.

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:54.510 position:63%
- [Gregor] Into these 10-micron holes.

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:56.303 position:63%
- [Krister] Into these 10-micron holes.

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:00.060 position:63%
- The photons then arrive
at two measurement stations

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:01.773 position:63%
110 meters apart.

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:04.350 position:63%
This means that even a signal

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:05.700 position:63%
traveling at the speed of light

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:07.170 position:63%
can't get from one to the other

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:09.630 position:63%
in time to impact the measurement.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:11.250 position:63%
Now, at each station,

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:13.770 position:63%
the researchers measure
the photon's polarization,

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:15.150 position:63%
but they can choose between two

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:17.130 position:63%
different angles to measure it at.

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:19.740 position:63%
And each station chooses
its angle at random

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.990 position:63%
just before the photon arrives.

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:26.820 position:63%
What sort of result are
you getting here and there

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:30.420 position:63%
that gives you that ultimate
final random number?

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:32.340 position:63%
- I either detect a photon or I don't.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:33.173 position:63%
- Okay.

00:30:33.173 --> 00:30:34.800 position:63%
A detection gives us a one.

00:30:34.800 --> 00:30:36.723 position:63%
No detection gives us a zero.

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:40.410 position:63%
But wait, they're using random numbers

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:42.570 position:63%
to choose the measurements
in an experiment

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:45.000 position:63%
that is designed to
generate random numbers.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.110 position:63%
It doesn't make sense.

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:47.910 position:63%
- How are these guys random

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:49.410 position:63%
if they don't use the whole setup?

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:52.980 position:63%
- So you know, it takes
randomness to make randomness.

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:55.017 position:63%
You just need pretty good randomness.

00:30:55.017 --> 00:30:57.450 position:63%
And this whole setup is a way to kind of

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:59.370 position:63%
purify or take, you know,

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:00.750 position:63%
what is pretty good randomness

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:02.370 position:63%
and turn it into something that is like

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:05.220 position:63%
certifiably like really good randomness.

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:07.770 position:63%
- The first numbers choose
which measurements to perform,

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:11.340 position:63%
but the numbers NIST wants to
certify are the zeros and ones

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:12.753 position:63%
coming out of the detectors.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:16.590 position:63%
Because each station has two
possible measurement angles,

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:19.770 position:63%
there are four possible
combinations of measurements.

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:22.080 position:63%
Now, if the photons were
simply carrying predetermined

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.510 position:63%
answers for every possible measurement,

00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:26.850 position:63%
there's a mathematical
limit to how strongly

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:29.760 position:63%
the results from the two
stations could be correlated.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.250 position:63%
But when the researchers
compare the results,

00:31:32.250 --> 00:31:35.010 position:63%
the correlations exceed that limit.

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:38.430 position:63%
This is called a violation
of Bell's inequality.

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:41.220 position:63%
We actually made an entire
video about Bell tests,

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:43.440 position:63%
and it goes much deeper into the topic.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:44.850 position:63%
So we've linked it in the description

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:47.190 position:63%
if you want to find out
more about how this works.

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:49.530 position:63%
Because the measurement
choices are independent

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:51.570 position:63%
and that the stations can't communicate

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:52.680 position:63%
during the measurements,

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:55.740 position:63%
that means the photons'
results can't be explained

00:31:55.740 --> 00:31:58.920 position:63%
by any kind of locally
predetermined outcome.

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:01.110 position:63%
So the experiment is actually testing

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:05.010 position:63%
whether those numbers contain
genuine unpredictability.

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:07.410 position:63%
But there are other potential loopholes.

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.030 position:63%
- The one that my own
group got involved with

00:32:09.030 --> 00:32:11.250 position:63%
was called the Freedom of Choice loophole.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:13.140 position:63%
Said no one should be surprised

00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:16.080 position:63%
if a student gets the quiz ahead of time,

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:17.520 position:63%
knows what questions she'll be asked,

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.200 position:63%
and then aces the quiz the next day.

00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:21.420 position:63%
These particles, again to speak
kind of anthropomorphically,

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:24.540 position:63%
shouldn't be allowed to know
what questions they'll be posed

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:27.000 position:63%
before they reach their detectors.

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:30.270 position:63%
And that goes back to
randomness really at its core.

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:31.740 position:63%
Can you really be confident that

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:34.200 position:63%
the selection of measurements to perform

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:37.410 position:63%
had no kind of correlations ahead of time?

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:38.700 position:63%
- [Gregor] There are
a few ways to do this,

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:41.910 position:63%
but David Kaiser and his
collaborators came up with this.

00:32:41.910 --> 00:32:43.920 position:63%
- What we wanted to do was
turn the universe itself

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:46.440 position:63%
into a pair of random number generators.

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:48.030 position:63%
- [Gregor] They pointed two telescopes

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:51.300 position:63%
at distant quasars on
opposite sides of the sky

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:54.180 position:63%
and used the wavelengths
from individual photons

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:56.220 position:63%
arriving from each of the quasars

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:58.800 position:63%
to choose which measurements to perform.

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:02.100 position:63%
Now, that light had been
traveling for billions of years,

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:04.620 position:63%
from regions of the universe that hadn't

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:06.630 position:63%
been able to communicate with each other

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:08.460 position:63%
since nearly the Big Bang.

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:10.950 position:63%
And assuming no one had
interfered with the light,

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:13.650 position:63%
any hidden coordination
between the measurement choices

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:15.750 position:63%
and the results would have had to happen

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:17.133 position:63%
billions of years ago.

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:20.640 position:63%
Now, this didn't completely
eliminate the loophole,

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:23.583 position:63%
but it pushed that possibility
much further back in time.

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:26.640 position:63%
David's team were investigating

00:33:26.640 --> 00:33:28.110 position:63%
whether some hidden explanation

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:29.640 position:63%
could account for the results,

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:31.980 position:63%
but NIST has to consider something else.

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:34.590 position:63%
- If I was a hacker, how
would I attack the system?

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:35.790 position:63%
What would I do?

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:37.890 position:63%
- [Gregor] In principle,
two people working together

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:40.350 position:63%
could shine their own light
into those telescopes,

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:42.840 position:63%
and control which
measurements are selected

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:45.150 position:63%
and potentially rig the results.

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:48.090 position:63%
So NIST has to keep the
random number generators

00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:49.890 position:63%
used to choose its measurements

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:52.380 position:63%
physically protected from
outside interference.

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:55.020 position:63%
They can't rely on quasar light for that.

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:57.300 position:63%
That makes it much harder for
someone to hack the system,

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:59.550 position:63%
but that doesn't prove their choices

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:02.070 position:63%
are independent of the
devices being tested.

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:04.260 position:63%
NIST still has to assume that,

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:06.240 position:63%
along with the impossibility

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:08.160 position:63%
of faster-than-light signaling,

00:34:08.160 --> 00:34:10.320 position:63%
accurate timing and distance measurements,

00:34:10.320 --> 00:34:13.890 position:63%
and faithful, secure
recording of the results.

00:34:13.890 --> 00:34:15.870 position:63%
If those assumptions hold,

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:17.910 position:63%
then the Bell test can certify

00:34:17.910 --> 00:34:19.980 position:63%
how much genuine unpredictability

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:21.633 position:63%
the detector outputs contain.

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:24.090 position:63%
- If we use entanglement,

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:26.160 position:63%
you can do these types of measurements,

00:34:26.160 --> 00:34:28.170 position:63%
and you can show that there's nothing

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:31.440 position:63%
that could have predicted
what's happening in advance.

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:33.360 position:63%
- [Gregor] But the raw
measurements aren't necessarily

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:36.060 position:63%
an even mixture of ones and zeros.

00:34:36.060 --> 00:34:38.460 position:63%
There might be far more zeros than ones.

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:40.320 position:63%
The Bell test tells the researchers

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:42.840 position:63%
how much unpredictability is present

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:45.120 position:63%
across millions of measurements,

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:47.400 position:63%
but it doesn't give them a neat sequence

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:50.790 position:63%
of evenly balanced,
independent, random bits.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:53.760 position:63%
So they need a way to get
that unpredictability out.

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:56.730 position:63%
The basic idea is similar to
the one von Neumann proposed

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:00.960 position:63%
in the same 1951 paper as
his "state of sin" joke.

00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:02.433 position:63%
You flip a coin twice.

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:06.780 position:63%
If you get heads, then
tails, you call that a zero.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:09.870 position:63%
And if you get tails, and then
heads, you call that a one.

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:13.380 position:63%
And if both flips match, then
you just throw them away.

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:14.670 position:63%
Even if the coin is biased,

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:16.950 position:63%
as long as the flips are independent

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:18.750 position:63%
and the bias doesn't change,

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:20.640 position:63%
heads-tails and tails-heads

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:21.870 position:63%
are equally likely.

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:24.360 position:63%
So what's left is perfectly fair.

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:26.820 position:63%
You just end up with a shorter sequence.

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:29.130 position:63%
Now, NIST's technique
is far more powerful,

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.200 position:63%
but the basic idea is similar.

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:34.800 position:63%
The Bell test certifies
at least 820 bits worth

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:37.410 position:63%
of unpredictability in
the raw measurements.

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:39.420 position:63%
And then using a mathematical technique

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:41.310 position:63%
called randomness extraction,

00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:45.423 position:63%
the researchers concentrate
that down to just 512 bits.

00:35:46.350 --> 00:35:49.050 position:63%
- Every one of the
measurements that we make

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:50.850 position:63%
contributes to every bit.

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:52.290 position:63%
- And unlike the random numbers

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:54.030 position:63%
that we've seen throughout the video,

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:56.460 position:63%
these aren't just difficult to predict.

00:35:56.460 --> 00:35:59.220 position:63%
Their unpredictability
can actually be certified.

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:02.640 position:63%
So this is the closest
you can get to random.

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:05.107 position:63%
- Albert Einstein, his argument was,

00:36:05.107 --> 00:36:07.230 position:63%
"God does not play dice
with the universe."

00:36:07.230 --> 00:36:09.630 position:63%
That's this famous Einstein quote.

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:11.910 position:63%
And Einstein was wrong about that.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.260 position:63%
In fact, these experiments

00:36:13.260 --> 00:36:15.570 position:63%
that we do here show that really,

00:36:15.570 --> 00:36:18.630 position:63%
you know, this predictable nature,

00:36:18.630 --> 00:36:20.280 position:63%
this kind of deterministic worldview

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:23.130 position:63%
that we have in classical
physics that Einstein espoused,

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:27.270 position:63%
really is incompatible
with quantum mechanics.

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:30.450 position:63%
So if God does play
dice with the universe,

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:32.340 position:63%
then what do those dice look like?

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:33.810 position:63%
And it's this experiment.

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:36.270 position:63%
You know, this is like God's
dice that we're throwing.

00:36:36.270 --> 00:36:39.693 position:63%
This is the most random source
of entropy you can generate.

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:42.397 position:63%
- So God's dice are not loaded.

00:36:42.397 --> 00:36:44.670 position:63%
- "God's dice are not loaded," exactly.

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:46.620 position:63%
Yeah, so this is something that, you know,

00:36:46.620 --> 00:36:49.800 position:63%
nothing in the universe could
have predicted in advance.

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:52.080 position:63%
- Together with the University
of Colorado Boulder,

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:56.760 position:63%
NIST actually publishes
this on random.colorado.edu,

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:58.860 position:63%
along with the information
that you need to trace

00:36:58.860 --> 00:37:01.200 position:63%
and verify how the numbers were generated.

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:03.000 position:63%
So you can go and use them if you want.

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:04.200 position:63%
These numbers have been shared,

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:08.010 position:63%
and I can really use the
bits that are generated here

00:37:08.010 --> 00:37:09.570 position:63%
to choose whether I'm wearing,

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:11.760 position:63%
I don't know, a green or a red sweater,

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:14.460 position:63%
and I'll be using these
entangled photons to do so.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:16.470 position:63%
- And I'm colorblind, so
it'll look the same to me

00:37:16.470 --> 00:37:18.150 position:63%
if you wear a red and green sweater,

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:20.940 position:63%
but producing the
randomness was really hard.

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:22.080 position:63%
It takes a lot of equipment.

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:23.040 position:63%
You've seen that today.

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:25.110 position:63%
We've figured out this process

00:37:25.110 --> 00:37:27.960 position:63%
of how we actually interconnect and weave

00:37:27.960 --> 00:37:29.430 position:63%
these different systems together,

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:30.960 position:63%
and these different beacons together.

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:33.000 position:63%
And sources of randomness

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:36.090 position:63%
can kind of actually form a
layer of additional security.

00:37:36.090 --> 00:37:38.330 position:63%
So yeah, I guess the thing
that we hope to randomize

00:37:38.330 --> 00:37:39.930 position:63%
is the internet.
- That's awesome.

00:37:39.930 --> 00:37:41.430 position:63%
- That's my ultimate dream is, you know,

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:44.968 position:63%
can we just make everything more secure?

00:37:44.968 --> 00:37:48.150 position:63%
(computer chirping)

00:37:48.150 --> 00:37:49.470 position:63%
- Okay, one last thing.

00:37:49.470 --> 00:37:52.800 position:63%
Last year we launched the
official Veritasium trivia game.

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:54.360 position:63%
It's called Elements of Truth.

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:56.190 position:63%
It's a game with 200 questions

00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:58.110 position:63%
about science and technology,

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:00.360 position:63%
but it's not just about
getting the answer right.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.310 position:63%
See there's a confidence
mechanic in the game

00:38:02.310 --> 00:38:04.380 position:63%
where you have to put down a point

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:06.300 position:63%
between 1 and 10 on your answer.

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:08.190 position:63%
So even if you do get the question right,

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:11.490 position:63%
you may lose out on points
if you're doubting yourself.

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.680 position:63%
The team has been play testing
this when we get together,

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:15.390 position:63%
and it has been super fun.

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:16.770 position:63%
If you missed the Kickstarter,

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:19.800 position:63%
I'm so excited to announce
that it's now available.

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:21.810 position:63%
If you're interested, we put
a link in the description

00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:23.430 position:63%
to the Elements of Truth website.

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:25.290 position:63%
So thank you for supporting this project,

00:38:25.290 --> 00:38:27.040 position:63%
and as always, thanks for watching.

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:30.980 position:63%
- [Matthew] And the way
